WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.897
When a tool cannot give an answer, first find out why.

00:00:03.897 --> 00:00:09.500
A completed search that finds no order is different from a search that never finished.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.985
Only the first tells you anything about the records that were checked.

00:00:13.985 --> 00:00:17.297
A timeout means the program stopped waiting.

00:00:17.297 --> 00:00:21.385
The service might be busy or temporarily unreachable.

00:00:21.385 --> 00:00:26.369
It does not tell you that a parcel is missing, delivered, or even late.

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:32.077
An edge case is a less common situation the program still needs to handle.

00:00:32.077 --> 00:00:41.933
Decide which problems could improve after a short wait, which require corrected information, and when to pass the question to a person.

00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:45.609
Elena asks whether her climbing rope was delivered.

00:00:45.609 --> 00:00:50.913
Scout contacts the carrier, but no reply arrives before the time limit.

00:00:50.913 --> 00:00:54.711
Imagine the tool simply returns an empty box.

00:00:54.711 --> 00:01:00.516
The assistant might treat it as a normal result and tell Elena her rope arrived.

00:01:00.516 --> 00:01:04.135
There is nothing in that box to support the claim.

00:01:04.135 --> 00:01:07.989
Some errors are worth a second try, and some are not.

00:01:07.989 --> 00:01:13.932
A timeout is usually temporary, so retry, waiting a little longer each time.

00:01:13.932 --> 00:01:20.621
A four twenty-nine means slow down, so wait as long as the API asks, then retry.

00:01:20.621 --> 00:01:26.949
Server errors in the five hundreds are often brief, so a couple of retries is reasonable.

00:01:26.949 --> 00:01:34.749
But a four oh four, tracking number not found, will fail the same way every time, so report it straight away.

00:01:34.749 --> 00:01:41.299
And a four oh one, a bad API key, needs a person on your team, not another attempt.

00:01:41.299 --> 00:01:46.131
Retry what might work next time, and report what will not.

00:01:46.131 --> 00:01:51.028
Instead, return a clear message saying the carrier lookup timed out.

00:01:51.028 --> 00:01:56.779
The application can allow a short wait and one further attempt in this example.

00:01:56.779 --> 00:01:59.654
Ask what changes on the second try.

00:01:59.654 --> 00:02:03.147
If a carrier record arrives, Scout can read it.

00:02:03.147 --> 00:02:08.383
If the same timeout returns, Scout still lacks a delivery status.

00:02:08.383 --> 00:02:13.518
Retries belong inside the tool, where your code can handle them quietly.

00:02:13.518 --> 00:02:16.008
Try the call up to three times.

00:02:16.008 --> 00:02:22.046
Catch only the temporary errors, like a timeout, and let everything else fail at once.

00:02:22.046 --> 00:02:30.210
Between tries, wait a little longer each time, which is called backoff, so a struggling server gets room to recover.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:38.106
If the last try fails too, raise a clear error, and the tool loop reports it to the model with the is error flag.

00:02:38.106 --> 00:02:43.036
Keep the total short, because a customer is waiting while you retry.

00:02:43.036 --> 00:02:48.168
Imagine calling a carrier and hearing silence until the line cuts off.

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:52.428
You cannot tell from that silence whether the parcel is moving.

00:02:52.428 --> 00:02:57.295
It tells you something about the enquiry, not what happened to the parcel.

00:02:57.295 --> 00:03:02.343
Try the enquiry once more after the agreed wait in this example.

00:03:02.343 --> 00:03:06.973
If the carrier replies, replace the error with the returned status.

00:03:06.973 --> 00:03:13.342
If the same timeout comes back, the second attempt has used time without adding evidence.

00:03:13.342 --> 00:03:19.437
Now imagine the carrier answered clearly that it did not recognise the tracking number.

00:03:19.437 --> 00:03:24.100
Waiting and sending the same number again is unlikely to help.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.848
Ask for a corrected number instead.

00:03:26.848 --> 00:03:30.254
The next step follows from the kind of failure.

00:03:30.254 --> 00:03:35.325
Put a limit on the attempts so Elena is not left waiting indefinitely.

00:03:35.325 --> 00:03:40.211
Tell her the carrier status could not be confirmed and offer a follow-up.

00:03:40.211 --> 00:03:46.765
If the number itself were invalid, waiting would accomplish nothing; ask for the correct number.

00:03:46.765 --> 00:03:52.227
Good recovery starts with preserving the meaning of the original failure.
